# Book 4 figures: (anchor 'H:<section heading start>[+k]', file, caption, alt text)
FIGS = [
 ('H:1.1 Wavelength', 'b4_wavelength.png', 'Figure 1.? — Wavelength shrinks with frequency. At 2.45 GHz it is 122 mm in air and 65 mm on a stripline board, about the length of a credit card; at 77 GHz it is under 4 mm.',
  'Log-log plot of wavelength in millimeters against frequency from 0.1 to about 150 gigahertz. A blue line for free space and a dashed red line for stripline with relative permittivity 3.55, lower by a factor of 1.88. Marked points: 2.45 gigahertz, 122 millimeters in air and 65 on the board; 28 gigahertz, 10.7 millimeters; 77 gigahertz, 3.9 millimeters. A shaded band notes that a tenth of a wavelength there matches trace and cable sizes.'),
 ('H:2.2 Reflection coefficient', 'b4_standing.png', 'Figure 2.? — Standing waves along a line. A matched load gives a flat voltage; a 100 Ω load on 50 Ω (VSWR 2) ripples between 2/3 and 4/3; an open end swings from zero to double.',
  'Plot of voltage magnitude relative to the incident wave against distance from the load, from 0 to 1 wavelength. Green flat line at 1 for a matched load. Blue curve rippling between 0.67 and 1.33 for gamma one third. Red curve swinging between 0 and 2 for an open end. Peaks repeat every half wavelength.'),
 ('H:3.2 Three elements', 'b4_lmatch.png', 'Figure 3.? — Two matches from 10 Ω to 50 Ω at 2.45 GHz, swept from their component values. The L-network (Q = 2) holds 10 dB return loss from 1.94 to 2.87 GHz; the pi network (Q = 5) only from 2.31 to 2.57 GHz.',
  'Return loss in decibels against frequency from 1.5 to 3.5 gigahertz. Blue curve for the L-network peaks at 2.45 gigahertz and stays above 10 decibels from 1.94 to 2.87. Red curve for the pi network peaks at the same frequency but is much narrower, above 10 decibels only from 2.31 to 2.57. A dashed line marks 10 decibels.'),
 ('H:4.3 The single-stub', 'b4_smith_stub.png', 'Figure 4.? — The single-stub match of Worked Example 4.2 on the Smith chart. The load (A) rotates 0.199 λ toward the generator to B, where the admittance is 1 + j1; a shorted λ/8 stub cancels the j1 and lands on the center.',
  'Smith chart with resistance circles and reactance arcs. Point A at 100 plus j50 ohms, normalized 2 plus j1, reflection 0.447. A thick blue arc runs clockwise along the constant-reflection circle to point B at normalized impedance 0.5 minus j0.5, which lies on the dashed green unit-conductance circle. A red arrow goes from B to the center O, labeled as the shorted eighth-wave stub adding minus j1.'),
 ('H:5.3 Mismatch', 'b4_mismatch.png', 'Figure 5.? — Mismatch uncertainty. A generator and a sensor that both have a VSWR of 1.5 can make a power reading wrong by +0.34 to −0.35 dB; the worse either match, the wider the band.',
  'Plot of possible power error in decibels against load VSWR from 1 to 3, with upper and lower bound curves for source VSWR 1.2 in green, 1.5 in blue, and 2.0 in red. The curves fan out from zero at VSWR 1. Marked points at load VSWR 1.5 on the blue curves: plus 0.34 and minus 0.35 decibels.'),
 ('H:6.1 The low-pass', 'b4_lowpass.png', 'Figure 6.? — Third-order low-pass responses. The Butterworth is flat and takes 18.1 dB off twice the cutoff; the 0.1 dB Chebyshev trades a small ripple for a steeper skirt.',
  'Transmission in decibels against normalized frequency from 0 to 4. A blue Butterworth curve rolls off smoothly from the cutoff at 1; marked points at 2 and 2.45 times cutoff show 18.1 and 23.4 decibels of attenuation. A red Chebyshev curve stays flat within 0.1 decibel to the cutoff, then falls faster than the Butterworth.'),
 ('H:7.5 The dipole', 'b4_dipole.png', 'Figure 7.? — Left: power patterns of a half-wave and a short dipole, both doughnuts with a null along the wire. Right: a short dipole\'s efficiency collapses as its length falls; at λ/20 with 2 Ω of loss, half the power heats the wire.',
  'Two panels. Left, polar plot with the dipole along the vertical axis: the half-wave dipole pattern in blue is a slightly narrower figure eight than the dashed red short-dipole pattern, both with nulls at 0 and 180 degrees. Right, radiation efficiency against dipole length from 0.02 to 0.2 wavelengths for 2 ohms of loss in blue and 1.5 ohms in green; marked points at a twentieth of a wavelength, 50 percent, and a tenth of a wavelength, 84 percent.'),
 ('H:8.2 Gain, beamwidth', 'b4_array.png', 'Figure 8.? — Array factor of 16 elements at half-wavelength spacing. A −90° phase step per element moves the 6.3° main beam from broadside to 30°, and the beam widens slightly as it scans.',
  'Array factor in decibels against angle from broadside, minus 90 to 90 degrees. Blue broadside pattern with its main lobe at 0 degrees, 6.3 degrees wide at minus 3 decibels, first sidelobes at minus 13 decibels. Red steered pattern with its main lobe at 30 degrees, about 7 degrees wide, with a similar sidelobe structure shifted.'),
 ('H:9.2 The Wilkinson', 'b4_wilkinson.png', 'Figure 9.? — The equal-split Wilkinson divider of Worked Example 9.1: two 70.7 Ω quarter-wave lines, 16.8 mm long at 2.45 GHz, and a 100 Ω resistor across the outputs.',
  'Schematic of a Wilkinson divider. Port 1 at 50 ohms on the left splits into two branches, each a 70.7 ohm quarter-wave line labeled 16.8 millimeters, leading to port 2 and port 3 at 50 ohms. A 100 ohm resistor connects the two outputs. A note explains the equal split and the resistor\'s role in isolation.'),
 ('H:10.1 Microstrip', 'b4_microstrip.png', 'Figure 10.? — Microstrip impedance against trace width on a 0.508 mm laminate with εr = 3.55. The three widths Chapter 9 needs are marked: 50 Ω at 1.14 mm, 70.7 Ω at 0.62 mm, 35.4 Ω at 1.90 mm.',
  'Characteristic impedance in ohms against width-to-height ratio on a log axis from 0.1 to 10. The blue curve falls from about 170 ohms at narrow widths to about 17 ohms at wide ones. Red points mark 70.7 ohms at ratio 1.22, 50 ohms at 2.24, and 35.4 ohms at 3.74, with widths in millimeters.'),
 ('H:11.2 Multisection', 'b4_multisection.png', 'Figure 11.? — Matching 50 Ω to 100 Ω with quarter-wave sections. One section stays below |Γ| = 0.1 over 37 % of the center frequency; two binomial sections double that, at twice the length.',
  'Reflection magnitude against frequency from 0.5 to 4.4 gigahertz. Blue single-section curve has a V-shaped null at 2.45 gigahertz and stays under 0.1 from 2.0 to 2.9. Red two-section binomial curve has a flatter, wider null and stays under 0.1 from about 1.57 to 3.33, labeled 72 percent exact, 74 percent by the formula.'),
 ('H:12.4 Temperature', 'b4_cavity.png', 'Figure 12.? — A 10 GHz cavity with a 1.28 MHz bandwidth, warmed by 50 K. Copper drifts 8.5 MHz, far out of its own passband; Invar drifts 0.6 MHz, less than half a bandwidth.',
  'Relative transmission in decibels against offset from 10 gigahertz in megahertz. A black resonance curve centered at 0 with a 1.28 megahertz bandwidth. A dashed green curve, Invar, shifted by 0.6 megahertz, nearly overlapping. A red curve, copper, shifted to 8.5 megahertz, completely separate from the original passband.'),
 ('H:13.2 The small-antenna', 'b4_chu.png', 'Figure 13.? — The Chu limit. As an antenna shrinks inside its sphere, its minimum Q climbs as 1/(ka)³ and its bandwidth falls. A 915 MHz antenna in a 20 mm sphere can reach at best about 3.5 %.',
  'Log-log plot of minimum radiation Q against ka from about 0.05 to 2. The curve falls steeply from thousands at small ka to below 1 at large ka. A red point at ka 0.384, Q 20.3, labeled 915 megahertz in a 20 millimeter sphere. A green point at ka 0.513 for 2.45 gigahertz in a 10 millimeter sphere.'),
 ('H:14.1 The Fresnel', 'b4_fresnel.png', 'Figure 14.? — The first Fresnel zone of the 10 km, 5.8 GHz link, 11.4 m in radius at mid-path. A ridge reaching 5 m above the line of sight intrudes deep into it and costs 11.3 dB.',
  'Side view of a 10 kilometer path. The line of sight is a horizontal line at height zero, surrounded by a shaded ellipse, the first Fresnel zone, 11.4 meters in radius at the middle, with a dashed inner ellipse for 60 percent clearance. A green ground line curves up by the earth bulge of 1.47 meters at mid-path. A brown triangular ridge at 5 kilometers rises to 5 meters above the line of sight.'),
 ('H:15.4 Circulators as duplexers', 'b4_circulator.png', 'Figure 15.? — A circulator as duplexer (Worked Example 15.3). Direct leakage through 20 dB of isolation is 10 dBm, but a 15 dB antenna return loss sends 15 dBm straight to the receiver.',
  'Diagram of a three-port circulator drawn as a circle labeled ferrite, with rotation from port 1 to 2 to 3. Port 1 on the left connects to a 30 dBm transmitter, port 2 at top to the antenna, port 3 on the right to the receiver. A red arrow under the circle marks direct leakage of 10 dBm. An orange note marks the antenna reflection of 15 dBm routed to the receiver.'),
 ('H:16.1 The far-field', 'b4_farfield.png', 'Figure 16.? — Far-field distance 2D²/λ grows with the square of antenna size. A 0.2 m horn at 10 GHz needs 2.67 m and fits indoors; a 1 m dish at 30 GHz needs 200 m.',
  'Log-log plot of far-field distance in meters against antenna dimension from 1 centimeter to about 3 meters, with a blue line for 10 gigahertz and a red line for 30 gigahertz, both rising with slope two. Marked points: 0.2 meter horn at 10 gigahertz, 2.67 meters; 1 meter dish at 30 gigahertz, 200 meters. A shaded band from 1 to 20 meters is labeled as fitting an indoor chamber.'),
 ('H:17.2 The lossy line', 'b4_feedloss.png', 'Figure 17.? — System noise temperature with the feed loss before or after the LNA (Worked Example 17.2). At only 0.5 dB of loss, putting the LNA first lowers the system temperature from 125.1 K to 85.7 K.',
  'System noise temperature in kelvin against feed loss from 0 to 3 decibels. A red curve for loss first rises steeply from about 85 kelvin to about 410 kelvin. A blue curve for the LNA first stays almost flat near 86 kelvin. Marked points at 0.5 decibels: 125.1 kelvin red and 85.7 kelvin blue.'),
 ('H:18.4 Rejection', 'b4_bandpass.png', 'Figure 18.? — The three-pole, 4 % Chebyshev filter of this chapter at 2.45 GHz. It rejects 23.4 dB at 2.60 GHz and 36.7 dB at 2.70 GHz; with microstrip resonators (Qu = 200) its passband sits about 1.7 dB lower.',
  'Transmission in decibels against frequency from 2.15 to 2.8 gigahertz. A blue band-pass curve with a flat top about 98 megahertz wide around 2.45 gigahertz, falling steeply on both sides. A dashed red curve 1.74 decibels lower shows the loss with resonator Q of 200. Marked points at 2.60 gigahertz, 23.4 decibels, and 2.70 gigahertz, 36.7 decibels.'),
 ('H:19.2 Shunt switches', 'b4_switch.png', 'Figure 19.? — Isolation of an RF switch against frequency. A series switch with 0.1 pF off-capacitance gives only 16.4 dB at 2.45 GHz; adding a shunt element that turns on raises it to about 50 dB.',
  'Isolation in decibels against frequency from 0.3 to 10 gigahertz. A blue curve for the series switch alone falls from about 35 decibels at low frequency to about 6 at 10 gigahertz, marked 16.4 decibels at 2.45. A red curve for series plus shunt falls from about 70 to about 39 decibels, about 50 at 2.45 gigahertz.'),
 ('H:20.1 Power in decibels', 'b4_levels.png', 'Figure 20.? — Level diagram of Worked Example 20.1. One decibel of cable after a 100 W amplifier burns 20.6 W; the 17 dBi antenna then lifts the EIRP to 66 dBm, about 4 kW.',
  'Step diagram of level in dBm. Amplifier output at 50 dBm, 100 watts; a red drop of 1 decibel in the cable to 49 dBm, 79.4 watts, labeled 20.6 watts of heat; a green rise of 17 dBi antenna gain to an EIRP of 66 dBm, about 4 kilowatts.'),
 ('H:21.1 The optimum load', 'b4_loadline.png', 'Figure 21.? — The load line of a 100 W, 50 V GaN amplifier with a 5 V knee. The voltage swings 45 V each way about the 50 V bias, setting Ropt = 45²/200 = 10.13 Ω.',
  'Drain current against drain voltage from 0 to 100 volts, with five idealized gray device curves that rise steeply up to a knee near 5 volts and then flatten. A red load line runs from 5 volts and 8.9 amperes down to 95 volts and zero current, through the bias point at 50 volts and 4.44 amperes. A blue arrow below marks a swing of 45 volts each way.'),
 ('H:22.2 Conduction angle', 'b4_conduction.png', 'Figure 22.? — Efficiency and output against conduction angle. Class A (360°) reaches 50 %, class B (180°) 78.5 %, class C at 120° 89.7 %, but that class C stage delivers 2.14 dB less than class B.',
  'Two curves against conduction angle from 360 down to about 20 degrees. A blue curve, ideal drain efficiency, rises from 50 percent at 360 degrees through 78.5 at 180 to 89.7 at 120 and toward 100 percent. A dashed red curve on a right-hand axis, output relative to class B, stays near 0 decibels down to about 180 degrees, then falls, minus 2.14 decibels at 120 degrees and steeply below.'),
 ('H:23.3 The Doherty', 'b4_doherty.png', 'Figure 23.? — Efficiency against back-off. At the 8 dB average of a typical OFDM signal, class B gives 31.3 % while an ideal symmetric Doherty gives 62.5 %, thanks to its second peak at 6 dB.',
  'Drain efficiency in percent against output back-off from 0 to 14 decibels. A blue class B curve falls steadily from 78.5 percent. A red Doherty curve dips slightly and returns to 78.5 percent at 6 decibels back-off, then falls more slowly. At 8 decibels, marked points show 31.3 percent for class B and 62.5 percent for the Doherty.'),
 ('H:24.1 The thermal path', 'b4_thermal.png', 'Figure 24.? — Temperatures along the thermal path of Worked Example 24.1. With 57 W flowing out to 40 °C air, the sink sits at 57.1 °C, the flange at 62.8 °C, and the junction at 108.4 °C.',
  'Bar chart of temperature for four nodes. Ambient air 40.0 degrees Celsius in green, heat sink 57.1 in blue, flange 62.8 in orange, junction 108.4 in red. Between the bars, labels give each thermal resistance and its temperature rise: sink 0.3 degrees per watt, plus 17.1; interface 0.1, plus 5.7; junction to case 0.8, plus 45.6.'),
 ('H:25.2 The cascade', 'b4_cascade.png', 'Figure 25.? — Noise figure building up through the receiver of Worked Example 25.1. With the LNA first the chain ends at 2.41 dB; with the filter first it ends at 3.91 dB.',
  'Cumulative noise figure in decibels after each of four stages. Blue line, LNA first: 1.00, 1.06, 2.38, 2.41. Red line, filter first: 2.00, 3.00, 3.89, 3.91. A note says filter first costs 1.5 decibels of sensitivity but may still be the right choice.'),
 ('H:26.1 Phase noise', 'b4_leeson.png', 'Figure 26.? — Leeson\'s model for the 2.4 GHz oscillator of Worked Example 26.1: −125.4 dBc/Hz at 1 MHz with QL = 20, and 6 dB worse when the resonator Q halves.',
  'Phase noise in dBc per hertz against offset from 1 kilohertz to 10 megahertz on a log axis. A blue line for loaded Q 20 falls at 20 decibels per decade, marked minus 105.4 at 100 kilohertz and minus 125.4 at 1 megahertz. A red line for loaded Q 10 runs 6 decibels higher, marked minus 119.4 at 1 megahertz. A dotted floor at minus 161.'),
 ('H:27.1 The radar equation', 'b4_radar.png', 'Figure 27.? — The radar echo falls as the fourth power of range. For the radar of Worked Example 27.1 it crosses the −98 dBm detection threshold at 7.31 km.',
  'Received power in dBm against range from 2 to 16 kilometers. A blue curve falls as range to the minus fourth, passing minus 103.4 dBm at 10 kilometers. A dashed red horizontal line at minus 98 dBm is the required level. The two cross at 7.31 kilometers, marked as the maximum range.'),
 ('H:28.3 Gain and the cascade', 'b4_lna_gain.png', 'Figure 28.? — Receiver noise figure against LNA gain, for the chain of Chapter 25. At 15 dB of gain the receiver reaches 2.41 dB; beyond about 20 dB, extra gain buys little and costs linearity.',
  'Receiver noise figure in decibels against LNA gain from 0 to 30 decibels. The blue curve falls from about 12 decibels at zero gain, through 2.41 at 15 decibels of gain, approaching the LNA\'s own 1 decibel, shown as a dotted line.'),
 ('H:29.2 The image', 'b4_freqplan.png', 'Figure 29.? — Frequency plan of a 2.45 GHz receiver with a 400 MHz IF and low-side LO at 2.05 GHz. The image at 1.65 GHz converts just as well as the wanted signal, and the half-IF spur sits only 200 MHz below it.',
  'Frequency axis from 0.5 to 4 gigahertz with arrows. Green IF at 0.40, red image at 1.65, black LO at 2.05, orange half-IF spur at 2.25, blue wanted signal at 2.45. A gray double arrow shows the 800 megahertz between image and wanted signal.'),
 ('H:30.1 The terrestrial', 'b4_linkbudget.png', 'Figure 30.? — The link budget of Worked Example 30.1 as a waterfall: 27 dBm out, 127.7 dB of path loss, two 25 dBi antennas, and −52.7 dBm received, 27.3 dB above the receiver\'s sensitivity.',
  'Waterfall bar chart of level in dBm. Transmitter 27 dBm, feeder minus 1, antenna plus 25, path over 10 kilometers minus 127.7, antenna plus 25, feeder minus 1, ending at minus 52.7 dBm. A dashed line marks the receiver sensitivity of minus 80 dBm, and a blue arrow marks the 27.3 decibel fade margin.'),
 ('H:31.2 The mismatched load', 'b4_vswr.png', 'Figure 31.? — What a mismatch does to the amplifier. At 1.5:1 only 4 % of the power returns; at 10:1, 66.9 % returns and the worst-case peak voltage is 1.82 times the matched value.',
  'Plot against load VSWR on a log axis from 1 to 20. A red curve, reflected power in percent, rises from 0 through 4 percent at 1.5 to 66.9 percent at 10. A dashed blue curve on the right axis, peak voltage factor 1 plus gamma, rises from 1 to 1.82 at 10, labeled 182 volts.'),
 ('H:32.2 Klystrons', 'b4_applegate.png', 'Figure 32.? — Applegate diagram of klystron bunching (illustrative). Electrons sped up at the buncher gap catch the ones slowed half a cycle earlier, and they arrive at the catcher gap in bunches.',
  'Distance along the drift tube against time in RF periods. Many straight lines start from the buncher gap at the bottom, each a single electron; blue lines for sped-up electrons are steeper, red lines for slowed electrons shallower. The lines converge in groups at the green catcher gap line, showing the bunches.'),
 ('H:33.2 Power density', 'b4_exposure.png', 'Figure 33.? — Power density in front of a 36 dBW sector antenna at 3.5 GHz. The general-public limit of 10 W/m² is met at 5.63 m in free space and 9.0 m with ground reflection; the occupational limit at 2.52 m.',
  'Log-log plot of power density in watts per square meter against distance in the main beam from 1 to 40 meters. A blue free-space line and a dashed red line 2.56 times higher with ground reflection. Horizontal lines for the general-public limit at 10 and the occupational limit at 50. Crossings marked at 2.52, 5.63, and 9.0 meters.'),
 ('H:34.2 Transmitter noise', 'b4_txnoise.png', 'Figure 34.? — Transmitter noise in the receive band (Worked Example 34.1). 45 dB of duplexer isolation brings −135 dBm/Hz down to −180 dBm/Hz, 9 dB below the receiver\'s −171 dBm/Hz floor, for 0.51 dB of desense.',
  'Level diagram in dBm per hertz. A red bar at minus 135 for transmitter noise at the antenna port, an orange arrow down to an orange bar at minus 180 after 45 decibels of isolation, and a blue bar at minus 171 for the receiver floor. A gray arrow marks 9 decibels below the floor, 0.51 decibels of desense.'),
 ('H:35.1 The class E', 'b4_classe.png', 'Figure 35.? — Ideal class E waveforms. The switch closes when its voltage has returned to zero with zero slope; the voltage peaks at 3.562 VDD and the current at 2.862 IDC, 99.7 V and 10.2 A in Worked Example 35.1.',
  'Two cycles of switch voltage in blue and switch current in red against phase from 0 to 720 degrees. The current flows as a skewed half-sine while the switch is on, 0 to 180 degrees, peaking at 2.862 and ending at 2. The voltage is zero then and rises as a hump while the switch is off, 180 to 360 degrees, peaking at 3.562 and returning to zero with zero slope.'),
 ('H:36.4 Outphasing', 'b4_outphasing.png', 'Figure 36.? — Outphasing: two constant-amplitude signals at ±φ add to 2 cos φ. Backing off 3, 6, and 10 dB needs angles of 44.9°, 59.9°, and 71.6°.',
  'Outphasing angle in degrees against output back-off from 0 to 14 decibels. The blue curve rises steeply from zero and flattens toward 90 degrees; marked points at 3, 6, and 10 decibels show 44.9, 59.9, and 71.6 degrees. An inset shows two equal vectors at plus and minus phi adding to a red resultant of length 2 cos phi.'),
 ('H:37.2 Efficiency and power-added', 'b4_pae.png', 'Figure 37.? — Power-added efficiency against stage gain, for a drain efficiency of 62.5 %. At 13 dB of gain PAE is 59.4 %; at 6 dB it falls to 46.9 %.',
  'Efficiency in percent against stage gain from 3 to 25 decibels. A dashed gray line at 62.5 percent for drain efficiency. A blue curve for power-added efficiency rises toward it, passing 46.9 percent at 6 decibels and 59.4 percent at 13 decibels.'),
 ('H:38.1 The idea', 'b4_dpd.png', 'Figure 38.? — The idea behind predistortion (illustrative model). The amplifier compresses, the predistorter expands by the same amount, and together they stay straight up to saturation, but not beyond it.',
  'Output amplitude against input amplitude, both normalized. A red curve for the amplifier alone rises linearly and then bends over toward 1. A dashed green curve for the predistorter rises and then bends upward. A blue line for the combination is straight up to 1 and then flat.'),
 ('H:39.4 Direct digital', 'b4_dds.png', 'Figure 39.? — How a DDS works. A phase accumulator adds a step M every clock and wraps around; a sine table turns each phase into one sample. The output frequency is M·fclk/2^N.',
  'A blue staircase rising by a fixed step each clock cycle and wrapping back to zero, for a 5-bit accumulator with step 3, over 40 clock cycles. Red dots show the sine-table output for each clock, lying on a faint red sine wave.'),
 ('H:40.2 Directivity error', 'b4_directivity.png', 'Figure 40.? — How coupler directivity limits a power reading. With return loss plus directivity of 35 dB the error is within +0.15/−0.16 dB; at 26 dB it widens to +0.42/−0.45 dB.',
  'Detector reading error in decibels against antenna return loss plus coupler directivity from 10 to 50 decibels. A blue upper bound and a red lower bound converge toward zero as the sum grows. Marked points at 26 decibels, plus 0.42 and minus 0.45, and at 35 decibels, plus 0.15 and minus 0.16.'),
 ('H:41.9 Raising', 'b4_boost.png', 'Figure 41.? — How rarely an OFDM envelope needs the boost. The instantaneous power exceeds 3, 6, and 9 dB above average 14 %, 1.9 %, and 0.04 % of the time; the handset of Worked Example 41.7 needs its boost 3.9 % of the time.',
  'Semilog plot of percentage of time against level above average power from 0 to 10 decibels. The blue curve, e to the minus k, falls from 37 percent at 0 decibels through 14 percent at 3, 1.9 percent at 6, and 0.036 percent at 9. A red point at 5.1 decibels, 3.9 percent, is labeled as the handset boost case.'),
]
